/* 说唱俑 · 鼓震落土（v4）— the passage from the 人物御龙帛画 (bohua) into the Eastern Han drummer-storyteller (Mia 2003.101).
   One discovery: the silk went into the tomb and fine earth is poured in over it, layer on layer, until the screen is rammed earth;
   then a drum sounds, and every beat knocks a piece of earth away: first the kicked-out foot, then the drum under his arm, the body,
   the raised drumstick — and on the heaviest beat the earth drops off his laughing face. The man who kept the dead company underground
   (事死如事生) comes out of the earth on his own drum.
   Beats (seconds of D = 13, see T):
   ① 0–1.45   the silk at rest (p 0 = bohua rest: wall + silk + EH_SHARED.bohuaRest shadow).
   ② 1.45–3.45 fine earth pours from above in streams, thickening; the fill rises from the bottom in a lumpy front and buries the silk
              (covered at 3.45 — the silk and the figure are never on screen together).
   ③ 3.45–4.7 still: the whole screen is rammed earth with its layers (a real photograph of an Eastern Zhou rammed-earth wall).
   ④ 4.7–9.6  THE MOMENT, on the drum: 4.7 a piece falls from the lower left → the kicked foot; 5.9 the drum and the arm holding it;
              6.85 and 7.3 (two quick beats) the body, then the raised drumstick; the heaviest beat at 8.45 drops the earth from his
              laughing face. Every beat jolts the earth (a few px), the pieces crack loose, tumble (rigid 3D, with thickness), shed
              crumbs and dust; the dust sinks.
   ⑤ 10.0–12.7 the rest of the earth lets go all round him (from him outwards) and the dust settles: wall + figure + floor shadow = p 1.
   Physics are closed-form (ballistic, fixed spin): every frame is a pure function of p. The figure is never moved or altered — it is the
   hung work itself (the DOM's own canvas copy), seen through the holes. Offline: _wip/t-shuochang/v4/ (soil.py, chunks.py, assets.py).
   Rest: the floor shadow (on #fx, above the hung work, figure knocked out) — EH_SHARED.shuochangRest (unchanged from v1). */
