/* 汝窑 · 同案 — the passage from 张择端《清明上河图》(bianjing, the hung window of the scroll) into the 汝窑青瓷无纹水仙盆 (NPM).
   One discovery: the window of the scroll we were looking at lies flat on a long lacquer table, and at the end of the same table
   stands the sky-blue basin. In a Song study one unrolled a scroll and handled a vessel at the same table (赵希鹄《洞天清录》).
   The scroll is rolled up on the table; the camera moves along the table to the basin, lowering until it sees the basin as the
   museum photograph does; the table melts into this room's wall.
   One rigid 3-D scene, one pinhole camera (no yaw, no roll): the table top, the scroll window and the rolls are true perspective;
   the basin and its shadow are the photograph and its real floor shadow, placed at a point on the table and scaled by 1 / depth
   (the photograph was taken from ≈ 23° above: fitted from the rim's ellipse; the camera ends at that elevation).
   Beats (seconds of D = 15, see T):
   ① 0–1.0    the scroll at rest (p 0 = bianjing rest: wall + the window at its rect [+ EH_SHARED.bianjingRest]).
   ② 1.0–5.2  the camera rises and tilts from straight above to 28°: the window turns out to lie flat on a dark wooden table,
              its rolls at both ends (the left one round the roller, holding the rest of the scroll); the wall becomes the table
              (1.0–2.4). The camera stays on the scroll: the basin at the left end of the same table is not in view yet.
   ④ 5.4–7.6  the scroll is rolled up while the camera holds still: the two rolls close towards each other on the table until they
              touch (true thickness); a beat; only then does the camera move (the owner: "先收起，再转到汝窑").
   ⑤ 8.0–13.4 the camera turns left along the table to the basin, closing in and lowering from 28° to the photograph's 23°;
              the room behind goes from the bianjing wall to this wall (9.0–12.6) and the table melts into it (11.6–13.4).
   ⑥ 13.4–16  rest = p 1 (wall + the basin at its rect + its floor shadow); title 13.7, label 14.2.
   Light: one soft window light from above, a little right and in front (the photograph's own light), there from the start:
   the table is a little brighter towards the front and right, the rolls are shaded by it.
   The table: one board of dark old wood — a photographed wood (Poly Haven "Dark Wood", CC0) graded to the lacquer LAC, grain along
   the long side, drawn per screen row in true perspective (texStrips); the front edge is the same board. No patch, no sweep; the glaze is only
   ever the photograph's own colour.
   Rest: the floor shadow (on #fx, the basin knocked out) — EH_SHARED.ruyaoRest for the next room. While a compare / the special is
   on nothing is drawn over the work; while reading, nothing inside the panel. */
