/* Special exhibit "casting" (吉金 · 块范浇铸).
   The hung tiger you is replaced, while the panel is open, by its clay piece-mould (six blocks + a pouring cup). The visitor tilts a
   crucible of molten bronze over the cup. The melt inside the crucible keeps a level surface: it only runs out over the lip when the
   tilt lifts the surface above it, so as the crucible empties you have to tilt further (weir flow Q ∝ h^1.5, the tilt is a damped
   spring with weight). The cup drains into the mould at a limited rate: pour faster than that and the cup brims over (spill → gas
   holes); pause or trickle and the rising front skins over and the next metal will not fuse (冷隔); stop for good before it is full and
   the top of the vessel is missing (没灌满). The seams of the mould glow from the bottom up as the metal rises; the pour sound rises in
   pitch like a filling bottle. Cool → knock the mould four times (it jiggles, cracks, then falls apart) → a golden new bronze with
   your defects and the mould's seam lines → fling the time slider through three thousand years to its patina → your casting steps
   aside and stands next to the original, which is hung again untouched.
   Two views share one renderer (scene space = main_cut.webp px): an overlay on the hung work (wide screens) and a stage canvas in
   the panel (narrow screens, where the panel hides the work). Assets: rooms/jijin/s_* (made by _wip/s-casting/make_assets.py). */
