/* Special exhibit "celadon_kiln" (汝窑 · 拉风箱烧天青).
   1 装窑: drag the raw-glazed basin down onto the six-spur stand inside the saggar (it snaps on with a clink; the lid saggar comes down).
   2 烧: pump the double-acting box bellows (风箱) by dragging its handle back and forth, holding A / D, or pressing 拉风箱 / Enter in rhythm.
     The handle is a weight on air: the faster you drive it, the harder the air pushes back (drag ∝ v|v|). Every full stroke is a breath
     for the fire; strokes at a steady pace build the draft up like pushing a swing (a stroke too quick or out of step is a weak breath).
     The flame runs the mantou-kiln path — up over the bag wall, along the dome, down through the saggars, out of the back flue and up the
     chimney — and its colour tells the heat (暗红 → 橙红 → 金黄 → 白亮).
   3 闷: at white heat slide the chimney damper (烟囱闸门) in. The fire starves, smokes and goes lazy: the kiln is in reduction and the iron in
     the glaze turns blue-green. With the damper in the heat also falls, so you must keep pumping.
   4 封窑 → cool → 开窑: the colour comes from your fire (示意): 米黄 (oxidised), 豆青 (half reduced), 月白 (reduced, not soaked hot enough),
     天青 (reduced at full heat); too cool and the glaze never melts (生烧).
   5 The basin comes out of the saggar and, cooling, crackles: 叮、叮 — the glaze shrinks more than the body. The hung basin is famous for
     having none (台北故宫: "天青純淨沒有開片").
   6 Drag it over (or 翻过来): its foot, six spur marks where it sat in the kiln. Yours and the original stand side by side in the panel,
     both turn together; on the wall (wide screens) the hung original can be turned over by hand at any time to show its own foot.
   Assets: rooms/ruyao/s_basin.webp, s_bottom.webp (the museum's view of the foot, same object), s_bottom_plain.webp (foot with the
   Qianlong poem painted out, for the visitor's basin). Made by _wip/s-celadon_kiln (Vision cut-out + inpaint). */
