/* Special exhibit "cobalt_blind_paint" (青花 · 看不见颜色地画).
   A bisque jar with the hung jar's own profile stands on a potter's turntable. You paint it with raw cobalt — and raw cobalt is grey-black:
   nothing on the jar tells you what blue it will be. The brush is a weight on a spring (it lags, it drags on the dry body); slow strokes and
   a brush held still put more pigment down (it pools), quick strokes go thin; the brush runs dry and must be dipped again. A wet stroke
   shines for a moment, then sinks into the body and goes matte: it is fixed. There is no undo (Ctrl/⌘ Z says so).
   Spin the turntable (drag beside the jar, flick it, A / D) and hold the brush still: a perfect ring, the way the band lines were drawn.
   罩釉: the raw glaze rises over the jar and hides the drawing almost completely. 入窑: a short closed-kiln moment (not a spectacle — the
   black-to-blue reveal is the transition's). 开窑: your own jar, fired: the blue is as deep as the pigment you laid, every edge has bled a
   little under the glaze, pooled spots have burnt to black-brown (like the "iron spots" on the hung jar). Yours and the original stand side by
   side in the panel; nothing goes on the wall. On wide screens the hung original takes a window light you can drag across its glaze.
   Text: room.json special {hints, how, labels, captions}. Sound: recorded qhu-* if listed (matched by name), else engine synth.
   Notes: _wip/s-cobalt_blind_paint/. */
