/* Special exhibit "drum" (说唱俑 · 跟他打鼓).
   You play the flat drum under his arm; he keeps time with his stick on the drum's wooden rim and tells his story over it.
   First he plays two bars himself (the pattern of room.special.pattern, 8 eighths a bar), then it is your turn: his rim clicks go on, you
   play the skin. Hits on time make him warm up: the tomb lamp burns higher on him, the tempo steps up through room.special.bpm (84 → 132,
   with inertia: he speeds up over a couple of seconds, and he leans a little toward your timing like a real partner). At the top the
   audience claps along; three bars there and he closes the piece. Hits off the pattern or missed beats pile up: at three he stops, the lamp
   sinks back to a low glow and he waits; hit the drum again and he counts you back in one bar, a step slower.
   The figure never moves (rule of the room): only light and sound. The light is multiplicative (lamp light over the photograph, masked to
   the figure's alpha, like the passage into this room), so nothing is painted on him; at rest and when idle the overlay is the identity.
   Physical feel: where you hit matters (the middle of the skin is deep, the edge is crisp: the membrane's modes), how you hit matters
   (a quick mouse/pen approach or a firm press hits harder), and holding the drum after the hit chokes the ring (a hand on the skin).
   Each hit also pushes the lamp's flame (a damped spring), so the light sways after a hard hit.
   Views: wide screens — an overlay canvas in #cw aligned to api.artRect() (the hung work is the cut main_cut.webp: main px − cut offset);
   narrow screens — a close-up of face, drum and kicked foot in the panel. Always: the round 拍鼓 control (keyboard: Enter / F / J).
   Sound: recorded shc-dong, shc-da, shc-tick, shc-clap, shc-end if the room's sound designer ships them; otherwise voices built on the
   core's context via EH.audio.out() (no own AudioContext). Sounds are scheduled ahead on the audio clock; hits are judged on the clock the
   visitor hears (AudioContext.getOutputTimestamp), so output latency does not count against them. */
