;
/* Special exhibit "wheel" (彩陶 · 转盘画彩).
   A slow wheel (慢轮) with a blank basin on it, seen from above like the hung work. Grab the wheel's rim and fling it: it keeps turning
   (inertia) and slows down (bearing friction + the drag of the brush). Press and hold inside the basin: the brush touches the turning clay
   and lays a band of slip. Physics of the line: pigment per unit length = flow / surface speed, so a slow wheel pools a thick blotchy line,
   a fast one draws thin and breaks into dry-brush gaps; the brush load runs out after about one outer ring, and lifting re-loads it
   (so a long ring needs a joint, as the thirteen rings on the original show). The hand is the pointer: a shaking hand wobbles the line.
   Each stroke is judged on lift (closed ring? even width? steady radius?) with the texts in room.special.captions.
   Wide screens: the hung basin on the wall sits on the same wheel and turns with it, rigidly about its centre dot: its thirteen rings
   stand still while the three arc groups and the rim pattern go round (the rings are what a turning wheel makes). Drag the basin on the
   wall to spin it as well. Narrow screens (the work hidden behind the panel): "把原作放上转盘" puts the original on the wheel instead of
   the blank (no brush on the original). "和原作并排" shows your basin next to the original's centre (crop from room.special.crop).
   Geometry: local units = main.webp px, centred on the centre dot (room.special.center); cut/layers.json gives the cut + shadow offsets.
   Sound: recorded cai-wheel (loop), cai-brush (loop), cai-flick, cai-new if the room's sound designer ships them; else voices built on
   the core's context via EH.audio.out() (no own AudioContext). */
